/* Plain-language text for every stop, written for someone meeting St John of the Cross for the first time.
   t: title. idea: John's point in everyday words. pic: what the scene's objects stand for.
   q: the instruction during play. items: [label, feedback] in the same order as the level. g: the one thing to remember. */
window.PLAIN=[
/* 0 */ {t:"The map: three roads up the mountain",
 idea:"This book explains a poem John wrote about a soul slipping out at night to meet God, the one it loves. He also drew a picture for it: a mountain with three roads. Two wide roads chase good things, of this world or of heaven, for their own sake, and never reach the top. Only the narrow middle road gets there. It is marked nada, Spanish for 'nothing': wanting nothing but God.",
 pic:"You walk into John's own drawing of the mountain. Step left or right at the foot to choose a road. On the two wide roads, good things float along the way and you take them by walking into them, but each one makes you smaller, and the road wanders off without reaching the top. The narrow middle road has nothing on it but the word nada.",
 q:"Choose a road, and find the one that reaches the top.",
 items:[["Goods of earth","A wide road: money, pleasure, know-how, comfort and rest, wanted for their own sake. John wrote on it: the more I looked for them, the less I had."],["Goods of heaven","Also wide: glory, good feelings, spiritual insight, comfort and rest, grabbed for yourself. Even holy things, grabbed, lead nowhere."],["Nada","The narrow road of 'nothing': letting go of everything that is not God. It is the only road that reaches the top."],["All without wanting","At the top John wrote: when I stopped wanting them, I had them all without wanting. Peace, joy, wisdom, strength and love come as gifts."]],
 g:"The way up is wanting God alone, not even his gifts."},
/* 1 */ {t:"Why John wrote this book",
 idea:"Many people who start to pray seriously reach a stage where prayer goes dark and dry. They think something has gone wrong, and the people advising them often agree. John writes to say that this darkness can be God leading them on. Don't fight it, and don't let bad advice push you back.",
 pic:"A night valley below the mountain. Three figures sit by warm fires beside the path, each calling you over with bad advice; give in and you lose the road. A guide with a lantern walks ahead, and at the cliff you can't climb, you are lifted up only when you stop struggling.",
 q:"Walk the valley toward the mountain. Keep to the path.",
 items:[["Only melancholy","Some advisers call God's dark night a mood."],["A hidden sin","Like Job's friends, some heap guilt on a soul already suffering."],["Old ways","Some push the soul back to ways of prayer it has outgrown."],["A guide","A wise guide knows this night, and walks it with you."],["Carried","Stop kicking, like a child whose mother wants to carry it."]],
 g:"Darkness in prayer is often God at work, not something gone wrong."},
/* 2 */ {t:"Two nights: the body and the soul",
 idea:"In John's poem a soul slips out of its house at night, while everyone inside is asleep. The house is you. The sleepers are your desires: until they are quiet, you cannot get away to God. John says there are two such nights. First the senses, the bodily side of you, are quieted. Later the spirit, your mind and heart. Part of this you do yourself; part God does in you.",
 pic:"Four people pace the house. Put them all to sleep at the same time (each one wakes again after a while). Then the door opens and you slip out.",
 q:"Put all four to sleep at once, then slip out.",
 items:[["Sight and hearing","Your desires keep 'the house' awake. They must be quiet before you can leave."],["Taste and touch","The first night quiets the bodily side of you. It is for beginners."],["Imagination","Part of this night is your own effort. Part is God's work in you, beyond your control."],["Reasoning","The second night quiets the mind and heart themselves. It is for those already close to God."]],
 g:"You can only go out to God when your desires are asleep."},
/* 3 */ {t:"Why John calls it 'night'",
 idea:"John calls the whole journey a night, in three ways. At the start it is dark because you give up the things you crave. Along the way it is dark because you travel by faith, trusting what you cannot see. At the end it is dark because God is too great for the mind to see. Think of one night with three parts: dusk, midnight and the hour before dawn.",
 pic:"The three nights of Tobias. At sunset burn the fish's heart and drive the demon off; at midnight walk the road you cannot see with your hand in Raphael's; before dawn lift your eyes to the dark sun.",
 q:"From which, by which, to which: three nights.",
 items:[["Dusk","The start: giving up your cravings. The things you leaned on fade from view."],["Midnight","The middle: faith. You trust what you cannot see, and the mind is in the dark."],["Before dawn","The goal: God himself, too bright for the mind to see. Yet the light is close."]],
 g:"Letting go, trusting and meeting God all feel like night."},
/* 4 */ {t:"It's the craving, not the things",
 idea:"'The night of the senses' does not mean hating the world or never enjoying anything. Things are not the problem; they never get inside you. The problem is the craving for them that lives in your heart. You can own very little and crave a lot, or have plenty and be free. The night falls on the wanting.",
 pic:"A table of good things, and a beast begging beside it. The beast is the craving. What do you do about it?",
 q:"What does the night of the senses ask of you?",
 items:[["Smash the table","No. Things themselves do no harm. Destroying them misses the point."],["Shut your eyes for good","No. Your senses are windows. You will keep looking through them, and that is fine."],["Stop feeding the craving","Yes. Leave the things where they are. Stop feeding the hunger inside."]],
 g:"Give up the clinging, not the world."},
/* 5 */ {t:"Why you have to let go",
 idea:"A heart cannot be full of two things at once. If you cling to something for its own sake, it fills the room God would fill. And love makes you like what you love: love something small and you become small. Even the best things people prize, beauty, wisdom, freedom, riches, are nothing next to God, who is all of them without limit.",
 pic:"Four bright braziers hide a great star overhead. Put out the small lights and the great one shows.",
 q:"Put out the small lights, then look up.",
 items:[["Beauty","A heart full of one thing has no room for another. Clinging fills the space meant for God."],["Wisdom","You become like what you love. Love something less than God and you shrink to its size."],["Freedom","Every good thing we prize is a tiny spark next to the same good in God."],["Wealth","Hold on to the small thing and your hands are too full to receive the great one."]],
 g:"You become like what you love, so love the greatest."},
/* 6 */ {t:"The Bible says the same",
 idea:"John shows the same lesson in Bible stories. Israel could not taste the heavenly manna while it still craved the food of Egypt. Before Jacob climbed to worship God at Bethel, he told his family to throw away their idols, wash and change their clothes. God wants an undivided heart, not one shared with other loves.",
 pic:"Jacob's camp below Bethel. Clear away everything that has to go, then climb to the altar.",
 q:"Clear the camp, then climb to the altar.",
 items:[["Strange gods","Throw away the idols: anything you love in a way that competes with God."],["Stained garments","Wash: clean away what those other loves left behind."],["Fleshpots of Egypt","Israel could not taste the manna while it longed for Egypt's food."],["A second fire","The altar holds one fire only. God will not share your heart."]],
 g:"God asks for an undivided heart."},
/* 7 */ {t:"Desires leave you empty and tired",
 idea:"Now John lists what clinging desires do to you. First, they take up the room where God would live. Then they wear you out. A desire is never satisfied: like a fire, the more you feed it, the bigger it grows. It is like a hungry man who dreams he is eating and wakes up still hungry.",
 pic:"A furnace that is never full. Throw in what it asks for, and watch what happens.",
 q:"Try to satisfy the fire.",
 items:[["One more pleasure","Desire is like a small child: never content, always wanting more."],["A little more","It is a fire that grows bigger the more you feed it."],["Just this once","It is like dreaming you are eating and waking up still hungry."],["And again","It is a cracked well that holds no water. You end up exhausted, with no room left for God."]],
 g:"Feeding a craving only makes it hungrier."},
/* 8 */ {t:"Desires torment you",
 idea:"Clinging desires don't only tire you; they hurt. John compares them to ropes tying you up and to thorns you try to lie on. The stronger the desire, the worse the pain. His picture is Samson, the Bible's strongman, who ended up blind and chained, grinding grain for his enemies. Jesus says: come to me, all you who are burdened, and I will give you rest.",
 pic:"You are Samson, blind in prison, pushing a millstone round. Each lap brings a new torment. Then someone calls at the door.",
 q:"Push the mill until someone calls you to stop.",
 items:[["Cords", "Desires bind like ropes. No rest until you're untied."], ["Thorns", "Resting on desires is lying on thorns. And fire runs through them."], ["The millstone", "Samson, strong and free, ended up blind and grinding for his enemies. Desires do the same."], ["The burden", "The more you crave, the heavier the load."], ["Rest", "“All you who thirst, come to the waters.” Here is rest."]],
 g:"What you crave ends up owning you."},
/* 9 */ {t:"Desires blind you",
 idea:"Desire clouds your judgment the way fog clouds a mirror or mud clouds water. When you crave something you cannot see clearly, about God or even about plain right and wrong. Desire is blind, and letting it lead you is the blind leading the blind, like a moth flying into the flame that dazzles it.",
 pic:"A cloister thick with fog. Clear away everything that blinds it.",
 q:"Clear what blinds this place.",
 items:[["Fog","A fogged mirror cannot show your face. A craving mind cannot see clearly."],["Mud","Stirred-up water hides what is underneath."],["Dazzle","The moth, dazzled by the flame, flies straight into it."],["Blind guide","When desire leads, it is the blind leading the blind into the pit."]],
 g:"Wanting something badly makes you see badly."},
/* 10 */ {t:"Desires stain you",
 idea:"A person is a beautiful image of God, like a fine face or pure gold. Clinging desires smear that image, like soot on a face or tar on your hands. Even one attachment you knowingly keep, though it is not a serious sin, leaves a mark that has to be cleaned before full union with God.",
 pic:"The Ark of the Covenant, where God dwelt among his people, smeared with tar and soot in a temple crawling with pests. Rub it clean and clear the temple.",
 q:"Clean the Ark and the temple.",
 items:[["Pitch","Handle tar and it sticks to you. Clinging leaves its mark."],["Soot","Soot on a beautiful face; cheap metal mixed into gold."],["Creeping things","The prophet Ezekiel saw creeping things painted on the temple walls: a mind busy with low things."],["Backs turned","He also saw men with their backs to God's sanctuary: a memory turned toward everything but God."]],
 g:"You are God's image; clinging smears it."},
/* 11 */ {t:"Desires drain your strength",
 idea:"Last, desires weaken you. A river split into many streams has no force. Hot water left uncovered goes cold. Your love works the same way: spread across many cravings it goes lukewarm, with no strength left for God. Gathered into one great love, it is strong.",
 pic:"A water wheel barely turns. Stop everything that is draining its strength.",
 q:"Stop what drains the wheel.",
 items:[["Many streams","Water split into many channels has no force."],["Open lid","Hot water left uncovered loses its heat."],["Suckers","Shoots round a tree's roots steal what should feed its fruit."],["Leeches","Desires drink your strength like leeches. Love gathered into one is strong."]],
 g:"A heart pulled many ways has no strength."},
/* 12 */ {t:"Even a thread holds the bird",
 idea:"Does every little want block you from God? No. Feelings that simply pop up, without your choosing, don't count. What holds you back is any attachment you knowingly keep, even a small one: a habit of chatter, a favourite object, a person you cling to. A bird tied by a thread can no more fly than one tied by a rope.",
 pic:"A bird strains against four cords: rope, cord, thread and hair. Cut every one, even the thinnest.",
 q:"Cut every cord, even the thinnest.",
 items:[["A rope","A big attachment obviously holds you down."],["A cord","So does a settled habit of small sins."],["A thread","So does one small attachment you choose to keep: an object, a habit, a person."],["A hair","Thick or thin, the bird cannot fly until it is cut. Feelings you never chose don't tie you."]],
 g:"A small attachment you keep holds you as surely as a big one."},
/* 13 */ {t:"Which desires do which harm",
 idea:"John clears up a worry. Only serious sin cuts you off from God's grace completely. But every desire you deliberately keep does some damage: it tires, hurts, blinds, stains and weakens you, in proportion to its strength. And temptations you feel but refuse actually make you stronger.",
 pic:"A night shore on the island of Patmos. A mighty angel stands with one foot on the sea and one on the land, holding the little book St John was told to eat: sweet in the mouth, bitter in the stomach. You carry a lantern. Sweet things wait on the path: take one, or walk on past it, and watch what happens to your light.",
 q:"Walk the shore to the angel. Take what you meet, or walk on past it.",
 items:[["Feelings you didn't choose","Thoughts and feelings that come without your consent do no harm at all."],["Sweet in the mouth","A desire you give in to tastes sweet while you're taking it."],["Bitter in the belly","The harm comes afterward. Every desire you choose to keep tires, hurts, blinds, stains and weakens you, in proportion to its strength, and each kind does one of these most: greed for food makes you lukewarm, greed for money makes you miserable, vanity blinds you, lust stains you."],["Power in weakness","Saying no to a temptation makes you stronger: 'my power is made perfect in weakness'."],["Only serious sin","Only a serious sin cuts you off from God's grace altogether. Smaller faults kept on purpose dim the light, each in its degree."]],
 g:"What you choose to keep harms you; what you refuse makes you strong."},
/* 14 */ {t:"How to begin: follow Christ",
 idea:"Here is John's practical method. First, want to live as Jesus lived, and read about his life so you know how. Second, for love of him, turn down any pleasure that doesn't serve God. Lean toward the harder, simpler choice, but sensibly. He sums it up in a famous verse: to come to have everything, want to have nothing.",
 pic:"Night and snow on the narrow road up the Mount. Christ goes ahead carrying his cross, and his footprints glow in the snow. Four times an easier road branches off, lovely and warm, but it runs round the mountain and never climbs. Near the top, the stones of nada: nothing, nothing, nothing.",
 q:"Follow in his footsteps to the top.",
 items:[["Imitate Christ","Want to live as Jesus lived. Read his life so you know how."],["Refuse the pleasure","For love of him, say no to any pleasure that doesn't serve God."],["Lean to the harder","Choose not the easiest but the hardest; not the most but the least."],["Desire nothing","'To come to have everything, desire to have nothing.'"],["With discretion","Do all this sensibly and in order, not in a frenzy."]],
 g:"Want what Christ wanted, and let the rest go."},
/* 15 */ {t:"Only a greater love can do it",
 idea:"Willpower alone won't get you through this night. The pull of the senses is strong, and only a stronger pull can beat it. John's poem says the soul went out 'on fire with love's longing'. You need to love God more than everything you are letting go of.",
 pic:"Pie-Eyed. You are crossing a kitchen at night toward the courtyard door, and the steam of a cooling pie has you by the nose. Pull against it and you only skid. Then someone calls from the courtyard.",
 q:"Get out to the courtyard.",
 items:[["Longing","Nobody gets through this night on willpower alone."],["Love of the Bridegroom","A deeper love, for God, gives the joy and strength to let everything else go."],["Courage","Without that love, no one would dare stay in the dark with nothing to lean on."]],
 g:"A greater love is what frees you from lesser ones."},
/* 16 */ {t:"The house is quiet: slip out",
 idea:"The poem calls the escape a happy chance. Since the Fall, says John, we have lived like prisoners of our desires. When they finally fall asleep, the soul can slip out unnoticed, free to go to God. That is the end of the night of the senses, and of Book One.",
 pic:"You are a prisoner. Settle the restless one, put the gaolers to sleep, put out the lamp, and walk out under the stars.",
 q:"Quiet the prison, then walk out.",
 items:[["Restless desire","Since the Fall we live as prisoners of our desires."],["The gaolers","Leaving unseen means no desire is awake to hold you back."],["The last lamp","When the house is quiet, the soul is free to go to the one it loves."]],
 g:"Quiet desires set you free to go to God."},
/* 17 */ {t:"Book Two: the secret ladder of faith",
 idea:"Book One quieted the senses. Book Two goes deeper, into the mind. The poem says the soul climbed out 'by a secret ladder, in disguise'. The ladder is faith: believing what God has said though you cannot see it. It is secret because neither your senses nor your reasoning can see its rungs, and safe because faith cannot be fooled the way your own ideas can.",
 pic:"A dark shaft with a ladder. Climb it, rung by lit rung.",
 q:"Climb the ladder of faith.",
 items:[["Hidden rungs","Faith is a ladder whose rungs you cannot see or reason out."],["Disguise","You stop knowing God by your own light and start knowing him by his."],["Safety","The more you go by faith, the harder you are to fool."],["Deeper dark","This night is darker: it switches off your mind's own light."]],
 g:"Faith is a ladder you climb without seeing the rungs."},
/* 18 */ {t:"Faith is midnight",
 idea:"Remember the three parts of the night? Giving up cravings was dusk. Faith is midnight, the darkest part, because it goes deeper. Now it is not only your senses that are left in the dark but your own reasoning, which has to trust what it cannot work out.",
 pic:"A terrace at night: a beacon, a reading lamp and your own lantern. Put them out, and see the moon.",
 q:"Put out the lower lights.",
 items:[["Dusk of sense","The first night only dimmed the outward, bodily side of you."],["Light of reason","Faith reaches further in, to your thinking mind."],["My own certainty","In this dark, the soul is made ready for union with God."]],
 g:"Faith darkens even your reasoning, because it trusts what reason can't reach."},
/* 19 */ {t:"Faith is like hearing about colours",
 idea:"Faith means believing what God has told us, truths too big for our minds. It is like a man born blind being told about colours. He can believe what he hears and use the words, but he has no picture of red. Faith is certain, because God said it, and dark, because you cannot see it.",
 pic:"A blind man is told about colours. Choose what he now has.",
 q:"What does the blind man now have?",
 items:[["A clear picture of red","No. He has never seen anything. He has no picture."],["Nothing at all","No. He has something real: the words, and trust in the one who told him."],["Names believed by hearing","Yes. That is faith: certain, because you trust who said it; dark, because you cannot see it."]],
 g:"Faith is certain and dark at the same time."},
/* 20 */ {t:"Trust the guide, not your glimpse",
 idea:"To be led by faith you have to stop relying on what you understand, feel or imagine about God. A blind man who trusts his guide gets there. A half-blind man who trusts his own glimpses over his guide gets lost. John's motto: to reach what you don't know, go by a way you don't know.",
 pic:"A half-blind man at a fork in a ravine. His guide points one way; a faint glimmer floats the other.",
 q:"Which way does he go?",
 items:[["Follow the glimpse","He gets lost. A little sight trusted over the guide is worse than none."],["Follow the guide","He arrives. To reach what you don't know, go by a way you don't know."]],
 g:"To reach the unknown God, go by the unknown way of faith."},
/* 21 */ {t:"What 'union with God' means",
 idea:"John's goal for the whole climb is 'union with God'. He doesn't mean anything strange. God is already in everyone, keeping them alive. Union means your will and God's will become one: nothing left in you pulls against him. His picture is sunlight on a window. Smeared glass blocks the light; clean glass seems to become light itself, though it is still glass.",
 pic:"Sunlight hits a smeared window. Rub the glass clean.",
 q:"Clean the window.",
 items:[["Self-will","Union means nothing left in you that pulls against what God wants."],["Old attachment","Cleaning is your part. Shining is God's."],["Last film","Clean glass looks like pure light, yet it stays glass. You stay you."]],
 g:"Union is a clean window full of God's light."},
/* 22 */ {t:"Three loaves for three hungers",
 idea:"Here is the plan for the rest of the book. You have three inner powers: your mind (which understands), your memory, and your will (which loves and chooses). God gives three gifts to match: faith for the mind, hope for the memory, and love for the will. Each gift empties its power of what it clings to, so that God can fill it.",
 pic:"A friend knocks at midnight asking for three loaves, as in Jesus' parable. Give each inner power its loaf.",
 q:"Give each power its loaf.",
 items:[["Mind","The mind is fed by faith: trusting what it cannot see."],["Memory","The memory is fed by hope: reaching for what it does not have yet."],["Will","The will is fed by love: love for God above every other love."]],
 g:"Faith for the mind, hope for the memory, love for the will."},
/* 23 */ {t:"The narrow gate",
 idea:"Jesus said the gate is narrow and the road is hard. John says you cannot get through carrying a load, not even a load of pleasant religious feelings. Chasing good feelings in prayer is really chasing yourself. Jesus did his greatest work on the cross, when he had nothing left. That emptiness is the way.",
 pic:"A narrow gate in a wall. Choose what to carry through: a chest of treasures, a glowing experience, or a cross.",
 q:"What can you carry through the gate?",
 items:[["Devotions and their sweetness","Too wide. Chasing good feelings in prayer is chasing yourself."],["Great experiences","Too wide. The road is not built of spiritual highs."],["Nothing but the cross","It fits. Jesus did his greatest work when he had nothing left."]],
 g:"You go through the narrow gate empty-handed."},
/* 24 */ {t:"No picture of God is big enough",
 idea:"To get somewhere you need a road that goes there. John says nothing you can picture or think is that road to God, because God is beyond every picture and idea. Next to him our minds are like a bat's eyes in sunlight. That is why the closest knowing of God feels dark: John calls it 'a ray of darkness'.",
 pic:"A cave mouth facing a blinding sun. Hold up a painting, a crystal-clear idea, or nothing at all.",
 q:"What can carry the mind all the way to God?",
 items:[["The finest image","No. Every picture is made of things you have seen. It holds only a hint of God."],["The clearest idea","No. Next to God the mind is a bat's eye facing the sun."],["None of them","Right. Nothing you can picture or think is big enough. God is known in a kind of darkness."]],
 g:"God is bigger than every picture and idea of him."},
/* 25 */ {t:"Faith: the light in the clay jar",
 idea:"If no picture or idea fits God, what does? Faith. Faith gives you God as he really is, though in the dark. John's picture: Gideon's soldiers hid their lamps inside clay jars, and the light appeared when the jars were broken. Faith is the jar; God is the light inside. When this life ends, the jar breaks and the light shines.",
 pic:"A ridge at night with clay jars hiding lamps. Break the jars.",
 q:"Break the jars.",
 items:[["Clay jar","Faith is the jar. It holds the light and hides it."],["Clay jar","What you believe and what the saints in heaven see is the same God."],["Clay jar","The more faith you have, the closer you are to God."],["This mortal life","When this life breaks, the light of faith shines out as glory."]],
 g:"Faith holds the real God, hidden like a light in a jar."},
/* 26 */ {t:"A map of what the mind can receive",
 idea:"John now sorts everything the mind can take in. Most knowledge comes the ordinary way, through the senses and thinking. Some comes in unusual ways during prayer: visions, messages, inner words, spiritual feelings. And one comes as a quiet, loving knowing of God in faith, which he calls contemplation. The rest of Book Two walks through this map, and the advice is always the same: don't stop here.",
 pic:"Three ways out of a map room: a lit window for ordinary knowing, a starry niche for unusual experiences, and a dark doorway for contemplation. Send each kind to its own.",
 q:"Sort each kind of knowing.",
 items:[["Senses and reasoning","The ordinary way: seeing, hearing, thinking."],["Visions","Unusual, and not the goal."],["Messages and inner words","Unusual, and not the goal."],["Spiritual feelings","Unusual, and not the goal."],["Contemplation in faith","The quiet, loving knowing of God that this whole book leads to."]],
 g:"Most of what the mind receives is a stop on the way, not the goal."},
/* 27 */ {t:"Visions and voices: let them pass",
 idea:"Sometimes people at prayer see lights, hear voices or smell sweetness. John's rule is firm: don't hold on to them, and don't even try to work out where they came from. They are easy to fake and easy to misread. If one really is from God, it has already done its good in you, so you lose nothing by letting it go.",
 pic:"A light hovers in the cloister with a sweet voice. Catch it in the net, weigh it on the scales, or walk on past to the far gate.",
 q:"What do you do with the light?",
 items:[["Hold on to it","Clinging to it weakens faith and makes you secretly proud."],["Test where it came from","No need. Stopping to judge it is already getting stuck."],["Let it pass","Right. If it is from God, its good is already done in you."]],
 g:"If an experience is from God, letting go of it loses nothing."},
/* 28 */ {t:"Pictures in prayer are stairs",
 idea:"Beginners pray by imagining scenes, such as Jesus on the cross or God as a great light. This is called meditation, and it is good. But pictures are like steps on a staircase: you climb them in order to leave them behind. Many people get stuck, straining to imagine when God is now inviting them simply to rest with him.",
 pic:"A staircase of holy pictures. Climb to each one; it sinks away behind you.",
 q:"Climb the stairs of pictures.",
 items:[["Christ at the pillar","Imagining holy scenes feeds love in beginners."],["Christ on the cross","This picturing is called meditation, and it is good."],["God as a great light","But every picture is made from things we have seen. None can hold God."],["The landing","No one arrives by standing on a step. In time, simply rest with God."]],
 g:"Use pictures to climb, then leave them behind."},
/* 29 */ {t:"Three signs it's time to move on",
 idea:"How do you know when to stop picturing and thinking in prayer, and simply rest? John gives three signs, and all three must be true together. One: picturing and thinking no longer work or satisfy. Two: you don't want to think about other things either. Three, the surest: you like to stay quietly with God, lovingly, without thinking about anything in particular.",
 pic:"Three candles, one for each sign. Each goes out after a while. Get all three burning at the same time.",
 q:"Light all three candles together.",
 items:[["Thinking doesn't work","Sign one. On its own, it could just be laziness."],["No wish for other thoughts","Sign two. Without sign three, it could just be low mood."],["Loving rest with God","Sign three, the surest. It is faint at first and grows."]],
 g:"All three signs together mean: rest quietly with God."},
/* 30 */ {t:"Why quiet prayer can feel like nothing",
 idea:"When you have pictured and thought about God for long enough, the love it was meant to produce becomes a habit, and more thinking is like peeling a fruit that is already peeled. The quiet that replaces it can be so pure you hardly notice it. A sunbeam is only visible because of the dust in it. Clear the dust and you can't see the beam, but the light is all there.",
 pic:"A sunbeam crosses the room, visible only because of its dust. Brush the dust out.",
 q:"Brush the dust out of the sunbeam.",
 items:[["Images","A sunbeam is visible only because of the dust in it."],["Reasonings","Clear the dust and the light is purer..."],["Feelings","...and now you can't see it at all."],["The sense of doing something","So you may think your prayer was wasted, when you were with God the whole time."]],
 g:"Pure prayer can feel like nothing happened."},
/* 31 */ {t:"When to go back to thinking",
 idea:"This quiet doesn't come every time at first. John's advice: when you can rest lovingly with God, stay there, and don't try to feel or think anything. When you can't, go back to thinking and picturing; that is fine too, until the quiet becomes a habit. 'Be still, and know that I am God.'",
 pic:"A dark room, light waiting outside. Open the shutters (they swing shut again if you stop).",
 q:"Open the shutters.",
 items:[["Straining to think","When the quiet comes, stay in it lovingly."],["Straining to feel","'Be still, and know that I am God.'"],["Fear of idleness","Clear your own effort away and the light comes in. When it doesn't come, go back to meditating."]],
 g:"Rest when the quiet is given; think when it isn't."},
/* 32 */ {t:"Pictures that appear on their own",
 idea:"Sometimes images appear in the imagination unasked, beautiful ones, like the visions of the prophets. They can come from God or be faked by the devil. John's rule: don't store them up. God is not in any picture. Whatever good an image carried was given the moment it came, so letting it go loses nothing.",
 pic:"A beautiful image floats in a storeroom. Put it in the cabinet, or let it go and follow the lamp.",
 q:"Where does the image go?",
 items:[["Into the storehouse","No. God is not in any picture. Stored, it becomes a possession."],["Let it pass, keep to faith","Right. Its good was given when it came. Faith is the lamp to follow."]],
 g:"Don't collect experiences; follow faith."},
/* 33 */ {t:"Why God gives them anyway",
 idea:"If visions should be let go, why does God give them? Because God leads people gently, the way they are made. We learn through our senses, so God starts there: with church, the Mass and holy things; then with imagination and prayer; then deeper, beyond anything we can sense. It is milk before solid food. Take the good inside and leave the peel.",
 pic:"Four thresholds lead inward, from a porch full of candles to a plain table. Go through them in order.",
 q:"Go inward, step by step.",
 items:[["Outer senses","God starts where we start: with what we see and hear, like church and the Mass."],["Imagination","Then prayer that pictures and thinks, and sometimes visions."],["Spirit","Step by step he draws you deeper than any sense can go."],["Leave the rind","Milk before solid food. Keep the good inside; leave the peel."]],
 g:"God starts with the senses to lead us beyond them."},
/* 34 */ {t:"Guides who love visions",
 idea:"John warns spiritual directors, the people who guide others in prayer. If a guide gets excited about someone's visions, that person learns to prize them too, and starts to feel special. Some guides even try to use such people to get answers from God. That is the blind leading the blind.",
 pic:"Someone kneels under a vision and tells you about it. You are their guide. On the table: a magnifying glass, a horn to ask for more, and a candle snuffer.",
 q:"You are the guide. What do you do?",
 items:[["Show great interest","They learn to prize visions, and to think they are special."],["Use it to ask God more","Now you have both left the road of faith."],["Make little of it","Right. Guide them by faith. Even true visions are easily misunderstood."]],
 g:"A good guide makes little of visions."},
/* 35 */ {t:"God's words mean more than they say",
 idea:"Even when God really speaks, people often misread him, because his words mean more than their surface. Abraham was promised the land and never owned it; his descendants did. People expected the Messiah to be a king on a throne; he came poor and reigned from a cross. Take God's words too literally and you will be wrong.",
 pic:"Three stone tablets carved with promises. Split each one to find the deeper meaning behind it.",
 q:"Split each promise open.",
 items:[["You will possess the land","Abraham died without it. The promise came true in his descendants."],["I will bring you back","Jacob died in Egypt. It was his people who came back."],["He shall reign","They expected a throne. He was born poor, and his throne was a cross."]],
 g:"God's promises come true, but deeper than we expect."},
/* 36 */ {t:"God's warnings can change",
 idea:"Some of God's words depend on what people do. Jonah announced that the city of Nineveh would be destroyed in forty days. The people repented, and God spared them. The word was true, and the people changed. So you can never be sure what a prophecy or a vision will turn out to mean. Faith is safer ground.",
 pic:"Jonah's warning hangs over Nineveh like a storm, and the people kneel at the gate. What happens?",
 q:"Nineveh repents. What happens?",
 items:[["The city falls","No. The warning depended on how they lived, and they changed."],["The city is spared","Yes. God's word was true, and it depended on them."]],
 g:"Many of God's words depend on our response."},
/* 37 */ {t:"God may answer and still be displeased",
 idea:"Sometimes God gives people the sign or answer they demand, but that doesn't mean he approves. It is like a father giving a crying child the one dish it screams for, while a better meal is ready. King Saul got a true answer from the dead prophet Samuel, and a rebuke with it. Demanding special signs leaves you open to being fooled.",
 pic:"A child cries for one dish while a feast waits behind a curtain. What does the father's giving in show?",
 q:"What does the father's giving in show?",
 items:[["He approves of the asking","No. Saul got his answer, and a rebuke with it."],["He stoops, and is not pleased","Right. God may answer demands for signs, but he doesn't want to be asked that way."]],
 g:"Getting an answer doesn't mean God liked the question."},
/* 38 */ {t:"God has said everything in Jesus",
 idea:"In Old Testament times it was right to ask God for signs. Now God has given us his Son, his one complete Word, and has said everything in him. John imagines God saying: 'I have told you everything in my Son. Look at him.' And God wants us to be helped by other people, through the Church and good guides, not by private messages.",
 pic:"Three small lights wander round a chapel where the Gospel lies open. Put out the small lights.",
 q:"Put out the lesser lights.",
 items:[["A new vision","In Old Testament times asking for signs was right: the full message had not yet come."],["A private answer","Now God has given his Son. In him he has said everything."],["A sign","Look at Jesus, and be guided by people and the Church."]],
 g:"Look at Jesus: God has nothing more to add."},
/* 39 */ {t:"Experiences of the spirit alone",
 idea:"Now John turns to experiences that skip the senses entirely and come straight to the mind. He names four kinds: things the mind 'sees' (visions), truths suddenly understood (revelations), words 'heard' inwardly (locutions), and something like touch or taste deep in the soul (spiritual feelings). They are higher than bodily experiences, but the same rule holds: don't cling.",
 pic:"Four plinths in a round room: an eye, a scroll, an ear and a hand. Carry each experience to its own.",
 q:"Match each experience to its kind.",
 items:[["Received like sight","A vision: something the mind 'sees'."],["A truth newly understood","A revelation: a hidden truth shown."],["Received like hearing","A locution: words heard inside."],["Received like touch or taste","A spiritual feeling. Higher than bodily experiences, but still not to be clung to."]],
 g:"Even the highest experiences aren't the goal."},
/* 40 */ {t:"What the spirit can see",
 idea:"In some visions the mind is shown real things in a special light: St Benedict is said to have seen the whole world in a single ray. But angels, souls and God himself cannot be clearly seen in this life: 'no one sees God and lives'. God can, though, be felt deep in the soul as a touch of love. Good visions leave peace and humility; false ones leave dryness and pride.",
 pic:"A rooftop under the stars: one arch open to the light (can be seen in this life), one veiled (cannot).",
 q:"What can be seen before death?",
 items:[["The whole world in one ray","Yes. St Benedict is said to have seen it this way."],["The heavenly Jerusalem","Yes. St John saw it in the Book of Revelation. Good visions leave peace and humility."],["Angels and souls as they are","Not in this life. Spirits cannot be clearly seen here."],["God's own being","No one sees God and lives. But he can be felt as a touch of love."]],
 g:"God is felt in love before he is seen in glory."},
/* 41 */ {t:"Two kinds of revelation",
 idea:"A revelation is a hidden truth being shown. John says there are two kinds. One is understanding a truth plainly, like suddenly grasping how good God is. The other is being shown a secret, like a hidden plan or something still to come. He takes each kind in turn next.",
 pic:"Two curtained alcoves: a lamp for truths understood, a key for secrets shown. Put each revelation in its place.",
 q:"Sort each revelation.",
 items:[["A sense of God's goodness","Understanding a truth. The next level is about this kind."],["A mystery disclosed","A secret shown."],["Insight into a heart","Understanding a truth, too."],["A promise or warning","A secret shown, too."]],
 g:"A revelation either shows a truth or uncovers a secret."},
/* 42 */ {t:"Knowing God, and knowing about things",
 idea:"Some sudden knowledge is a direct touch of God: a deep sense of his goodness or power that cannot be put into words. This is part of union itself, and only God can give it, so here John says: receive it humbly. Other knowledge is about things and people, like knowing what someone did in secret. That kind can be faked, so let it go and tell your guide.",
 pic:"A mountain ledge: a bowl held up to receive from above, and a cairn of stones to leave things on.",
 q:"Sort each kind of knowing.",
 items:[["A touch of God's goodness","Part of union itself. Only God can give it. Receive it humbly."],["A stranger's hidden deed","The prophet Elisha knew his servant's secret. But this kind can be faked."],["A sudden sense of God's power","It comes unasked. Don't grab at it."],["An event foreseen","Tell your guide and let it go."]],
 g:"Welcome touches of God; let go of special information."},
/* 43 */ {t:"No new secrets about the faith",
 idea:"Some revelations claim to show secrets about God or the future. John is firm: everything needed for faith has already been given to the Church. Even if a revelation repeats something true, believe it because the Church teaches it, not because of the revelation. The devil mixes truth with lies, like pushing a stiff bristle through leather so the soft thread can follow.",
 pic:"On one side, a star balanced on a pole. On the other, a church built on rock. Lean your faith on one.",
 q:"What should your faith rest on?",
 items:[["More firmly, because it was shown","No. Then your faith rests on the vision, not on God."],["As before: the Church teaches it","Right. Everything needed for faith has already been given."]],
 g:"Faith rests on what God gave the Church, not on private revelations."},
/* 44 */ {t:"Three kinds of inner words",
 idea:"Sometimes in prayer a person 'hears' words inside. John sorts them into three kinds: words your own mind puts together while you pray; words that come clearly from outside yourself; and words that actually do what they say, so that if God says 'be at peace', you are.",
 pic:"Three lecterns marked with a feather, a scroll and a hammer. Carry each kind of inner word to its own.",
 q:"Sort the inner words.",
 items:[["Words my own mind forms","Your mind puts them together while you pray."],["Clear words from outside me","Received from someone else."],["Words that do what they say","They change you as they are spoken."]],
 g:"Not every inner word comes from God."},
/* 45 */ {t:"Am I talking to myself?",
 idea:"Deep in prayer, your thoughts can flow so easily it feels as if someone else is speaking. Usually it is your own mind, helped by God's light, but adding conclusions of its own. John is blunt: many people who say 'God told me' were mostly talking to themselves. Make little of these words, and put your energy into humble love and good work.",
 pic:"Blind Isaac's tent. 'The voice is Jacob's, but the hands are Esau's.' Whose words are these really?",
 q:"Whose words are they?",
 items:[["God told me this","Mostly you were talking to yourself, and adding your own conclusions."],["My own mind, helped by light","Right. Make little of them. Put your energy into humble love and good work."]],
 g:"Be humble about the words you hear in prayer."},
/* 46 */ {t:"Clear words from outside",
 idea:"Sometimes clear words come into the mind from outside, whether you are praying or not. When they are from God and ask something great, you often feel reluctant, as Moses did when God sent him to Pharaoh. Words from the devil do the opposite: they make you eager for importance. Either way, don't act on them without asking a wise adviser.",
 pic:"A burning bush gives you an order. Grab the crown, rush down the road, or take it to the confessor's hut.",
 q:"What do you do next?",
 items:[["Promises honour; I feel eager","A warning sign. The devil's words make you hungry for greatness."],["Act on it at once","No. Never act on words like these alone."],["Ask a wise adviser","Right. God's real calls often leave you reluctant, as Moses was."]],
 g:"Never act on an inner voice without wise advice."},
/* 47 */ {t:"Words that do what they say",
 idea:"The rarest inner words don't just tell you something; they make it happen. If God says 'don't be afraid', fear leaves. If he says 'love me', love fills you. God's speaking is God's acting, as when Jesus spoke and people were healed. You cannot fake these and don't need to do anything with them. Just receive them humbly.",
 pic:"A man cowering, a dead tree, a cold hearth. Speak each word and watch it happen.",
 q:"Speak each word.",
 items:[["Fear not","And the man stands up, full of courage and peace."],["Be good","And the tree blossoms. God's speaking is his doing."],["Love me","And the fire catches. One word like this does more than a lifetime of effort."]],
 g:"When God speaks, it happens."},
/* 48 */ {t:"Touches of God, and the end of Book Two",
 idea:"Last, John describes deep feelings of God's touch in the heart or the very core of the soul. From them, a sweet sense of God overflows into the mind. The mind must not grab at it or try to add to it, or its own busyness will push out what God gave. Book Two ends where it began: humility, and a mind kept empty for faith.",
 pic:"A fountain overflows. Work the pump to add to it, or leave an empty basin to be filled.",
 q:"What does the mind do with the gift?",
 items:[["Grasp it and add to it","Your own effort crowds out what was given."],["Stay still and receive","Right. Humble, open, and empty for faith."]],
 g:"Receive God's gifts; don't grab them."},
/* 49 */ {t:"Book Three: memory and will",
 idea:"Book Two cleared the mind with faith. Book Three does the same for the other two inner powers: the memory, which is healed by hope, and the will (your love and choices), which is healed by love for God. John says he isn't attacking ordinary prayer. He is writing for people God is now leading into quieter prayer.",
 pic:"A harbour with a great anchor for hope and a heart for love. Carry each thing to its place.",
 q:"Carry each to hope or to love.",
 items:[["Memory","The memory is healed by hope."],["Will","The will is healed by love."],["What I possess","Hope lets go of what it already holds."],["What I enjoy","Love gathers every joy and gives it to God."]],
 g:"Hope for the memory, love for the will."},
/* 50 */ {t:"Letting your memory go quiet",
 idea:"Everything you see, hear, taste and touch leaves pictures in your memory. God has no shape for the memory to hold, so to meet him your memory must let go of all these pictures. In practice: don't store up and replay what you see and hear. Let it go, while still remembering what your duties need. God finishes the work.",
 pic:"A boat tied to the quay by four ropes. Cut them and let it drift out into the fog.",
 q:"Cut the ropes.",
 items:[["Things seen","God has no shape your memory can hold."],["Things heard","Don't store up and replay what you see and hear."],["Tastes and touches","At first this can make you forgetful, even of meals."],["Old stories","Later God himself reminds you of what you need, when you need it."]],
 g:"Let go of the replay; keep your memory free for God."},
/* 51 */ {t:"Harm one: a busy memory stirs you up",
 idea:"Constantly replaying memories causes harm. The first harm comes from the world: you misjudge things, and you get stirred up with worry, fear, resentment, false hopes and pride. John's remedy is to quiet the memory, and all of these calm down together.",
 pic:"Fire pits in a locked room keep flaring up. Slam the lids shut (they spring open again if you are slow).",
 q:"Slam the lids shut.",
 items:[["False judgment","Turning things over, you start treating guesses as facts."],["Sorrow and fear","What you dwell on stirs up these feelings."],["Empty joy","And pride comes with it."],["Wasted time","Quiet the memory and they all calm down. Christ came through locked doors to bring peace."]],
 g:"Dwelling on memories stirs everything up."},
/* 52 */ {t:"Harm two: a door for the devil",
 idea:"John says the devil works mostly through what your memory stores. He adds to the pictures and thoughts already there, and uses them to stir up pride, greed, anger and envy. He can make false things feel true. Keep your memory quiet and you shut the door: he has nothing to work with.",
 pic:"The devil stands on a plank propped up by three piles of stones. Knock the piles away.",
 q:"Knock out his footholds.",
 items:[["Added images","He adds to what your memory already holds."],["Pride and anger","Through those pictures he stirs up your feelings."],["False made true","He can make lies feel true."]],
 g:"A quiet memory gives the devil nothing to grip."},
/* 53 */ {t:"Harm three: lost peace",
 idea:"A wandering memory also robs you of peace. Your feelings are stirred by what you dwell on, and a stirred-up soul cannot receive what God gives; his gifts settle only on a soul at peace. John's advice: trade what changes and can be grasped for the one who never changes.",
 pic:"A cistern leaks through three cracks. Plug them and let the water rise.",
 q:"Plug the leaks.",
 items:[["Old grievances","Your feelings are stirred by whatever you dwell on."],["Daydreams","God's gifts settle only on a soul at peace."],["Rehearsed worries","Trade what changes for the one who never changes."]],
 g:"Peace is what lets God's gifts settle in."},
/* 54 */ {t:"What a quiet memory gives",
 idea:"The rewards are the opposite of the harms: peace of mind, a clear conscience, protection from temptation and openness to the Holy Spirit. John adds plain common sense: worry has never fixed anything, and usually makes things worse. Meet everything with the same calm.",
 pic:"An olive tree at night with four lamps hanging in it. Light each one.",
 q:"Light the lamps.",
 items:[["Peace of mind","Free of the thoughts that upset you."],["Clean conscience","Peace brings a clear conscience with it."],["Safety","Temptation comes in through thoughts."],["Readiness for the Spirit","Worry never fixed anything. Meet everything calmly."]],
 g:"Worry never fixed anything."},
/* 55 */ {t:"Even holy memories",
 idea:"Visions and special moments in prayer leave memories too. John's rule is the same: don't keep going back to them. Hope is about what you don't have yet. The more your memory lets go, the more room there is for hope, and the more of God you have.",
 pic:"A soul kneels, tied by golden cords to a vision, a word and a comfort on the shelves. Cut the cords.",
 q:"Cut the golden cords.",
 items:[["A vision recalled","Don't keep going back to it."],["A word once heard","Holding on to what is clear and particular leaves less room for faith."],["A consolation","Hope is for what you don't have. The more you let go, the more you hope."]],
 g:"Letting go of even holy memories makes room for hope."},
/* 56 */ {t:"Replaying experiences fools you",
 idea:"If you keep going back over special experiences, you will often get them wrong: mistaking your imagination for God, or God's work for the devil's, or making them bigger or smaller than they were. Since you can't be sure, the sensible thing is not to try to judge them. Tell a good guide and let them go.",
 pic:"A crossroads in fog. The bright road says 'from God', the masked road says 'from the devil', the way straight on says 'let it go'.",
 q:"Where did the experience come from?",
 items:[["Certainly from God","You may be mistaking your imagination for God's voice."],["Certainly from the devil","Or mistaking God's work for the devil's."],["I cannot be sure, and I let it go","Right. All such experiences together are worth less than one humble act."]],
 g:"You don't need to judge every experience. Let it go."},
/* 57 */ {t:"Replaying experiences makes you proud",
 idea:"Treasured experiences can make you feel special, like the Pharisee in the Temple who thanked God he wasn't like other people. You grow impatient when others don't admire you, and jealous when they are given the same. Real goodness isn't in feelings. It is in humility, which you usually can't even feel.",
 pic:"The Pharisee stands in the Temple under his banners; a humble man waits at the back. Strike the banners down and see who goes home right with God.",
 q:"Strike down the Pharisee's banners.",
 items:[["I am favoured","Like the Pharisee thanking God he wasn't like other people."],["Impatience","With anyone who doesn't admire you."],["Envy","When others are given the same. Real goodness is humility, which you can't feel."]],
 g:"Real holiness is humble, not impressive."},
/* 58 */ {t:"The devil as an angel of light",
 idea:"The devil can plant false memories that look holy; the Bible says he can disguise himself as an angel of light. He makes you so attached to the sweetness of what you remember that it blinds you. If you refuse to dwell on any of it you are safe: he can't use what you won't keep.",
 pic:"A glowing figure like an angel. Sink into the soft seat before it, or shut the heavy door on it.",
 q:"What do you do?",
 items:[["Dwell on its sweetness","That is how it blinds you. The devil can look like an angel of light."],["Refuse to keep it","Right. He can't use what you won't keep."]],
 g:"What you don't keep can't be used against you."},
/* 59 */ {t:"Clinging memories block hope",
 idea:"Your memory reaches God through hope, and hope is about what you don't yet have. So anything the memory clutches stands in the way. Jesus said: whoever does not give up all they have cannot be my disciple. That includes what you hold in your memory.",
 pic:"A kite pinned to a hilltop by three strings. Cut them and let it rise.",
 q:"Cut the strings.",
 items:[["What I know","Your memory reaches God through hope."],["What I have felt","Hope is about what you don't have yet."],["All that I have","'Whoever does not give up all they have cannot be my disciple.'"]],
 g:"What the memory clutches, it cannot hope for."},
/* 60 */ {t:"Making God too small",
 idea:"If you prize your experiences and pictures of God, you end up measuring God by them, and so you think of him as smaller than he is. John's picture: a servant at a royal court who pays attention to the courtiers instead of the king. The more he admires them, the less he honours the king.",
 pic:"A royal court: chattering courtiers on one side, the king on his throne ahead. Where do you look?",
 q:"Where do you look?",
 items:[["At the courtiers","The more you notice them, the less you honour the king."],["At the king","Right. Nothing compares with God. Prizing pictures of him makes him small."]],
 g:"Look at God, not at your experiences of him."},
/* 61 */ {t:"What letting go gains",
 idea:"Letting go of experiences brings peace, and spares you the endless job of deciding which ones were real. Isn't that rejecting God's gifts? No, says John: their good is given in the moment, and your own grasping adds nothing. But you may remember an experience for the love it stirred, as long as you don't cling to its shape.",
 pic:"A threshing floor. The wind through the arch blows the chaff away; the basket keeps the grain.",
 q:"Wind or basket?",
 items:[["The shape of a vision","Let the wind take it. Its good was given at the time."],["The love it awakened","Keep it. You may remember for the sake of love."],["Sorting true from false","Let the wind take it. You are freed from that job."],["Peace","Keep it. This is the gain."]],
 g:"Keep the love, let go of the shape."},
/* 62 */ {t:"Memories with no pictures",
 idea:"Some knowledge leaves no picture, only an inner sense. If it was a deep touch of God, remember it as often as you can, not by its shape, since it has none, but by what it did: the light, love and renewal it brought. If it is about other things, remember it only when it moves you to love.",
 pic:"A lamp-lit niche for what is worth remembering, and a stream to carry the rest away.",
 q:"What is worth remembering?",
 items:[["A touch of union","Remember it often, by what it did: light, love, renewal."],["Knowledge that stirs love","Remember it when it moves you to love."],["Knowledge that stirs nothing","Let the stream take it."]],
 g:"Remember what moves you to love."},
/* 63 */ {t:"One rule for the memory",
 idea:"John's rule: whenever a memory or picture comes up, don't settle into it. Turn straight to God, lovingly, with an open, empty memory. Think about things only as much as your duties need. And this isn't against holy images: the Church uses them rightly. An image is a doorway. Go through it to the real person it shows.",
 pic:"Three pictures hung like doors. Push each one open and go through.",
 q:"Go through each picture.",
 items:[["Painted image","Images rightly move us to prayer. They are doorways."],["A distinct memory","Don't settle in it. Turn straight to God."],["A remembered form","Think about things only as much as your duties need."]],
 g:"Pass through every picture to God."},
/* 64 */ {t:"The will and its four feelings",
 idea:"Now the will: your power to love and choose. The great command is to love God with all your heart and all your strength. Your strength flows out through four basic feelings: joy, hope, sorrow and fear. When you are glad, hope, grieve and fear only for God's sake, your strength is gathered. When they run loose, every fault grows from them.",
 pic:"Four weathervanes, each carrying a flame, all pointing away from the fire. Swing each one round to face it.",
 q:"Turn each feeling toward the fire.",
 items:[["Joy","Be glad only in what honours God."],["Hope","Hope for nothing else."],["Sorrow","Grieve only over what touches this."],["Fear","Fear only God. Where one feeling goes, the others follow."]],
 g:"Aim joy, hope, sorrow and fear at God."},
/* 65 */ {t:"Six kinds of good things",
 idea:"John starts with joy, the will's delight in something it values. He lists six kinds of good things people enjoy: worldly goods like money and status; natural gifts like looks and talent; pleasures of the senses; good deeds; special gifts like healing; and spiritual things. One rule covers all six: be glad only in what gives honour to God.",
 pic:"Six terraces climbing toward a sunburst, one for each kind of good. Climb through them.",
 q:"Climb through the six kinds.",
 items:[["Worldly goods","Money, status, family."],["Natural gifts","Looks, health, talent."],["Pleasures","What the senses and imagination enjoy."],["Good deeds","Virtues and good works."],["Special gifts","Gifts like healing, given to help others."],["Spiritual things","What brings God to the soul. One rule for all six: be glad in what honours God."]],
 g:"Be glad only in what honours God."},
/* 66 */ {t:"Joy in money and family",
 idea:"Riches, status, jobs and even family are reasons for joy only when they help you serve God. Money doesn't make anyone better; more often it leads people astray. Jesus called riches thorns. St Paul's advice: those who have things should live as if they didn't.",
 pic:"A counting house: a heart for what is given away, a strongbox for what is hoarded. Put each coin where it belongs.",
 q:"Heart or strongbox?",
 items:[["Being rich","Strongbox. Riches don't make you a better person. Jesus called them thorns."],["Wealth spent in God's service","Heart. This is a real reason for joy."],["Gifted children","Strongbox. Their gifts matter only if they serve God."],["Having as though having not","Heart. St Paul's advice."]],
 g:"Have things as though you didn't."},
/* 67 */ {t:"How money pulls you away",
 idea:"John traces four steps by which loving possessions takes you away from God. First your thinking about God goes dull. Then you drop prayer and go through the motions. Then greed leads you into serious wrong. Finally money becomes your god, and you serve it instead of it serving you.",
 pic:"Four terraces lead down from a height toward a golden calf. Take each step and watch the light go.",
 q:"Follow the four steps down.",
 items:[["Dulled mind","Your judgment clouds. Gifts blind even the wise."],["Slack will","Prayer is dropped; duties become routine."],["God abandoned","Greed leads into serious sin."],["Money as god","You serve money. 'This very night your life will be demanded of you.'"]],
 g:"Loving money moves you away from God, one step at a time."},
/* 68 */ {t:"What freedom from possessions gives",
 idea:"Letting go of joy in possessions makes you generous, like God himself. It brings freedom, a clear head and peaceful trust. And you actually enjoy things more, because you see them as they are, not as things to own. St Paul says: 'having nothing, and yet possessing everything'.",
 pic:"Four locked chests in a courtyard. Open each one and let what is inside fly free.",
 q:"Open the chests.",
 items:[["Generosity","Like God himself."],["Freedom","A free spirit and a clear mind."],["Truer enjoyment","You enjoy things as they are, not as things you own."],["The hundredfold","Having nothing, yet possessing everything."]],
 g:"The free person enjoys things more."},
/* 69 */ {t:"Joy in looks and talent",
 idea:"Beauty, charm, health and intelligence are gifts, not achievements. To be pleased with yourself for having them is empty, and they can easily lead you or others astray. Thank God for them and let them point you to him, the source of all beauty.",
 pic:"A dressing room: a mirror on one side, a window to the sky on the other.",
 q:"What do you do with your gifts?",
 items:[["Delight in having them","Empty. 'Charm is deceptive and beauty fades.'"],["Refer them to God","Right. All beauty comes from him, without limit."]],
 g:"Your gifts point to the giver."},
/* 70 */ {t:"How vanity harms you",
 idea:"Taking pride in your gifts brings six harms: vanity and looking down on others; self-indulgence; flattery; a dulled conscience; scattered attention; and finally a lukewarm, half-hearted faith. John says almost no one escapes this danger completely. Samson, David and Solomon all fell to it.",
 pic:"A banquet table with six brimming cups. Tip out each one.",
 q:"Tip out each cup.",
 items:[["Vanity","Pride, and looking down on those without your gift."],["Self-indulgence","Your senses grow comfortable and greedy."],["Flattery","Empty praise is a kind of lie."],["Dulled spirit","Reason and conscience grow blunt."],["Scattered mind","Your attention spreads thin."],["Lukewarmness","Half-hearted faith. Even Samson, David and Solomon drank from this cup."]],
 g:"Pride in your gifts makes your faith lukewarm."},
/* 71 */ {t:"What humility about your gifts gives",
 idea:"Not resting in your own gifts brings humility, and a love for others that looks past appearances to the real good in them. Your senses stay calm, your imagination stays clean, and you are free to follow Christ.",
 pic:"A round temple with four bronze doors. Push each one open and let the light in.",
 q:"Open the doors.",
 items:[["Humility","Toward yourself."],["Love for all","Free of appearances, you see the real good in each person."],["Guarded senses","A calm, clean imagination."],["Purity","Fit for a temple of the Holy Spirit."]],
 g:"Not resting in your gifts frees you to love others."},
/* 72 */ {t:"Pleasures of the senses: the test",
 idea:"What about enjoying music, beauty or good food? John gives a simple test. If, the moment you enjoy something, your heart rises straight to God, and the pleasure helps it rise, then use it. If the pleasure just stops at itself, it gets in the way, however holy it looks.",
 pic:"A harp plays. Sink into the soft couch beside it, or rise into the light above.",
 q:"Music moves you. Where does the moment go?",
 items:[["The pleasure rests in itself","Then it holds you back, however devout it looks."],["The heart rises straight to God","Then enjoy it. That is the test."]],
 g:"If a pleasure lifts you to God, enjoy it."},
/* 73 */ {t:"When the senses run wild",
 idea:"Indulged, each sense brings its own trouble. The eyes: vanity and envy. The ears: gossip and judging others. Smell: distaste for the poor and for humble work. Taste: greed and coldness to the needy, like the rich man who ignored Lazarus at his gate. Touch does the most harm of all.",
 pic:"Five snarling hounds round a feast, one for each sense. Quiet each one.",
 q:"Quiet each hound.",
 items:[["Sight","Vanity, envy, impure thoughts."],["Hearing","Gossip, envy, judging others."],["Smell","Distaste for the poor and for humble work."],["Taste","Greed, anger, ignoring Lazarus at the gate."],["Touch","The worst: softness, lust, inconstancy."]],
 g:"Each sense, indulged, has its own way of pulling you down."},
/* 74 */ {t:"What calm senses give",
 idea:"When you stop chasing pleasures of the senses, your scattered energy comes back. You become a spiritual person instead of one driven by appetite. Then, surprisingly, everything you see and hear starts lifting you to God. 'To the pure, all things are pure.'",
 pic:"A walled garden with four closed buds. Wake each flower.",
 q:"Wake the flowers.",
 items:[["Recollection","Your scattered strength comes back."],["A spiritual person","You stop being driven by appetite."],["All things pure","Everything you see and hear lifts you to God."],["Glory","And a joy to come that far outweighs what you gave up."]],
 g:"Calm senses find God everywhere."},
/* 75 */ {t:"Joy in your good deeds",
 idea:"Good deeds and virtues really are good; even philosophers before Christ praised them. But for a Christian, a good deed counts for eternity only if it is done for love of God. Jesus told of five foolish bridesmaids with fine lamps and no oil: they did the right things, without the love.",
 pic:"Five handsome lamps on a ledge, unlit. Reach for the polishing cloth, or for the jar of oil?",
 q:"What are the lamps missing?",
 items:[["Nothing: the works are good","Good deeds are good. But the foolish bridesmaids did good things too."],["Oil: the love of God","Right. Good deeds count for eternity when they are done for love of God."]],
 g:"Do good for love of God, not for yourself."},
/* 76 */ {t:"How pride spoils good deeds",
 idea:"Being pleased with your own good deeds spoils them, in seven ways: boasting; looking down on others; doing good only when you are noticed (John mocks donors who want their name carved on their gift); losing God's reward; giving up when it stops feeling good; thinking the deeds you enjoy are the best ones; and refusing advice.",
 pic:"A long stairway down, with a banner at each step. Each banner falls as you pass it.",
 q:"Follow the seven harms down.",
 items:[["Boasting","Like the Pharisee."],["Judging others","Thinking they are worse than you."],["Working for praise","A name carved on the gift. 'They have received their reward.'"],["Reward lost","Nothing left to receive from God."],["No progress","When it stops feeling good, they stop."],["Self-deception","Thinking the deeds they enjoy are the best ones."],["Deaf to advice","And cooling in love."]],
 g:"Good deeds done for show lose their worth."},
/* 77 */ {t:"Doing good quietly",
 idea:"Not taking pride in your good deeds protects you from temptations hidden inside them, and makes you steadier, with no burst of enthusiasm that soon fizzles out. It gives you the 'poverty of spirit' that Jesus called blessed. You become gentle, humble and wise.",
 pic:"Four coins on a table and a poor box. Give each coin where no one sees.",
 q:"Give each coin in secret.",
 items:[["Freedom","From temptations hidden inside good deeds."],["Steadier work","No burst of enthusiasm to fizzle out."],["Poverty of spirit","Which Jesus called blessed."],["Meekness","Gentle, humble and wise in what you do."]],
 g:"Good done quietly stays good."},
/* 78 */ {t:"Joy in special powers",
 idea:"Some people are given special gifts like healing, prophecy or working miracles, meant to help others. John says these are no reason for personal joy, and without love they are worthless. When the disciples came back thrilled that demons obeyed them, Jesus said: rejoice instead that your names are written in heaven.",
 pic:"The disciples come home. Put each cause for joy by the book of life, or on the heap of broken chains.",
 q:"What is worth being glad about?",
 items:[["Spirits obey you","Not this. Jesus told them not to rejoice in it."],["Your names are written in heaven","This is the reason for joy."],["Healing and prophecy","Not this. Without love these gifts are nothing."],["God is served in charity","The one benefit worth joy."]],
 g:"Be glad that God knows you, not that you have powers."},
/* 79 */ {t:"Showing off special powers",
 idea:"Delighting in such gifts brings harm. You are easily fooled, and tempted to use them at the wrong time, which the devil loves. Faith suffers too: miracles done when no one needs them make faith cheaper, and you start leaning on signs instead of trust. And pride comes with it.",
 pic:"A crowd waits in the square. Raise the staff that could become a serpent and amaze them, or sit down on the stone.",
 q:"Do you work the sign?",
 items:[["Work it","Signs nobody needs cheapen faith and feed pride."],["Refrain","Right. God gives signs when people truly need them to believe."]],
 g:"Faith doesn't need a show."},
/* 80 */ {t:"Trusting without proof",
 idea:"Turning your heart away from signs and wonders honours God: it says he is enough. 'Be still, and know that I am God.' And it lifts you up too: God gives himself more fully to people who trust him without proof. Faith grows, and hope and love grow with it.",
 pic:"Three signal fires burn under a cloud that hides the peak. Beat them out and watch the cloud part.",
 q:"Beat out the signal fires.",
 items:[["Signs","'Be still, and know that I am God.'"],["Testimonies","God gives himself more to those who trust without proof."],["My own gift","Faith grows, and hope and love grow with it."]],
 g:"Trust without proof, and God gives himself more."},
/* 81 */ {t:"Spiritual good things",
 idea:"The highest of the six kinds are spiritual goods: things that draw you toward God. John divides them. Some feel good and some are painful; some are clear and some are vague and dark. The painful ones belong to his other book, The Dark Night. The dark kind is deep, wordless prayer, which he promised to explain at the end of this book and never reached. Here he covers the pleasant, clear ones.",
 pic:"A table with a basket for what John covers here, and a shut cupboard for what he leaves for later.",
 q:"Which does John cover here?",
 items:[["Pleasant and clear","Covered here: images, chapels, ceremonies and preaching."],["Painful","Left for his other book, The Dark Night."],["Deep, dark prayer","Promised for the end of this book, but he never got there."]],
 g:"John turns to the everyday helps to devotion."},
/* 82 */ {t:"Joy in visions, one more time",
 idea:"Some spiritual good things are the visions, messages and feelings already covered in Book Two. John doesn't repeat himself: the will treats them exactly as the mind and memory were told to. Don't take joy in them for their own sake; keep your joy for God.",
 pic:"A bowl brimming with lights. Clutch it to yourself, or pour it out on the altar.",
 q:"What does the will do with them?",
 items:[["Rejoice in them now","No. The will follows the same rule as the mind and memory."],["Pour the joy out to God","Right. Keep your joy for God himself."]],
 g:"Keep your joy for God, not for experiences."},
/* 83 */ {t:"Holy pictures and statues",
 idea:"Now the things that stir up devotion: holy pictures, chapels and ceremonies. Images of Christ and the saints are good; the Church gives them to honour the saints and to move our hearts. So the best image is the one that moves you most toward God, not the most expensive. John mocks people who dress statues in the latest fashions, or fuss over what their rosary is made of.",
 pic:"An image-maker's shop: a prayer niche for what helps devotion, a market stall for the rest.",
 q:"What makes a holy image good?",
 items:[["One that moves me to God","The best image is the one that draws you most to God."],["Costly workmanship","Adds nothing to devotion."],["A statue dressed in fashion","John mocks it."],["The material of a rosary","God doesn't hear your prayer better because of it."],["Few images, used well","A devout person needs only a few."]],
 g:"The best image is the one that brings you closest to God."},
/* 84 */ {t:"Why some shrines seem special",
 idea:"Sometimes favours are granted at one particular statue or shrine. John says the power isn't in the image but in the faith of the people who pray there. God often chooses plain images in out-of-the-way places, so the effort of the journey deepens love and the quiet helps prayer. Trusting one image over another, as if God were tied to it, is a mistake.",
 pic:"A plain statue in a hilltop shrine, and pilgrims kneeling who walked a long way to reach it.",
 q:"Why are prayers answered here?",
 items:[["The statue has power","No. God isn't tied to any image."],["The faith of those who pray","Right. The journey deepens love, and the quiet helps prayer."]],
 g:"God answers faith, not statues."},
/* 85 */ {t:"How to use a holy image",
 idea:"Use an image like a signpost: let it send your mind straight to the person it shows, Christ or a saint. Don't let your eyes just linger on it, whether it is carved or painted, beautiful or plain. Used this way, an image can't mislead you or get in the way.",
 pic:"Three framed images. Strike each one and it rolls up like a blind, showing a window behind.",
 q:"Let each image open onto the one it shows.",
 items:[["Carved image","Don't let your eyes linger on it."],["Painted image","Beautiful or plain, let it send your mind on."],["Inward image","Used this way, it can't mislead you."]],
 g:"An image is a signpost: follow where it points."},
/* 86 */ {t:"Pretty chapels and empty hearts",
 idea:"Some people enjoy decorating their place of prayer so much that the decorating becomes the point, with little love of God in it. John recalls Jesus entering Jerusalem: crowds waved palms and sang, while he wept over the city, because their hearts were far from him. 'This people honours me with their lips.'",
 pic:"A street of palms and banners, a splendid arch ahead, and one person kneeling quietly. What is Christ looking at?",
 q:"What is Christ looking at?",
 items:[["The splendour of the feast","Their hearts were far away. Many celebrations are for those celebrating."],["The heart","Right. 'This people honours me with their lips, but their hearts are far from me.'"]],
 g:"God looks at the heart, not the decorations."},
/* 87 */ {t:"Where to pray",
 idea:"Beginners can be helped by a beautiful church, the way a child is helped by something to hold. But to grow you have to let that attraction go. Jesus told the woman at the well that God wants worship 'in spirit and truth', and your own soul is God's temple. The best place to pray is the one that distracts your senses least. Jesus himself prayed on bare hills.",
 pic:"The woman at the well asked which mountain is the place to pray. A splendid temple on one side, a bare hill on the other.",
 q:"Where do you go to pray?",
 items:[["The most splendid temple","Fine for beginners. In time you have to let that attraction go."],["A bare hill, in spirit and truth","Right. Jesus prayed in lonely places. Your soul is the real temple."]],
 g:"Pray where your senses are least busy."},
/* 88 */ {t:"Stop chasing the feeling",
 idea:"People who depend on a particular place or feeling to pray never settle into real inner quiet. They keep changing places, devotions and even their whole way of life, and stick with nothing, because they are led by taste. John's advice: keep a clean conscience, give God your whole will, and keep your mind set on him.",
 pic:"A tent in the desert, and a road of signposts that loops back to it. Chase a new devotion, or stay and turn inward.",
 q:"The feeling has gone. What now?",
 items:[["A new place, a new devotion","And then another. Led by taste, you stick with nothing."],["Stay, and turn inward","Right. Real inner quiet means letting go of those flavours."]],
 g:"Real prayer doesn't depend on the feeling."},
/* 89 */ {t:"The cost of chasing the feeling",
 idea:"Seeking pleasant feelings in devotion leaves you distracted and dry inside, and unable to pray anywhere but your favourite spot. It also makes you restless: people take up a new way of life with excitement, like becoming a hermit, then drop it when the excitement fades.",
 pic:"Four whirligigs spin beside a candle stand. Still each one (they start again if you let them).",
 q:"Still each whirligig.",
 items:[["Distraction","Inwardly, you are scattered."],["Dryness","And dry in spirit."],["Only here","Unable to pray except in the favourite place."],["Restlessness","Starting something new, and dropping it when the excitement fades."]],
 g:"Chasing feelings leaves you dry and restless."},
/* 90 */ {t:"Three kinds of holy places",
 idea:"John names three kinds of holy places. Beautiful natural places that lift the heart: use them, but forget the place once you start praying. Places where God once gave you a special grace: it is fine to go back sometimes, without clinging. And places God himself chose, like Mount Sinai.",
 pic:"A quiet grove, a standing stone, and a peak marked by cloud. Carry each place to its kind.",
 q:"Sort the holy places.",
 items:[["Open land, trees, quiet","Use it, then forget the place once you pray."],["Bethel","Jacob set up a stone where God met him."],["Sinai","God chose it for himself."],["Abraham's altar","Built where God appeared to him."],["Monte Gargano","An Italian mountain linked to St Michael."]],
 g:"Places can help prayer, but God isn't confined to them."},
/* 91 */ {t:"When prayer becomes superstition",
 idea:"Some people believe a prayer only works with exactly so many candles, said by a certain priest at a certain hour, in a fixed number and posture, and that if one detail is missing nothing will happen. John says this trusts the method more than God, which insults him, and it opens the door to being fooled.",
 pic:"One flame on an altar, ringed by a candle rack, an hourglass, a tally frame and a posted rule. Knock the rules away.",
 q:"Knock the rules away and see what is left.",
 items:[["Exactly so many candles","No more and no fewer."],["Only this priest, this hour","Or it won't count."],["A fixed count and posture","For every prayer."],["Or nothing will come of it","Trusting the method instead of God insults him."]],
 g:"Trust God, not the method."},
/* 92 */ {t:"How Jesus taught us to pray",
 idea:"So how should we pray? Trust God himself, with no rituals beyond the Church's own. Ask first for what matters most: 'seek first the kingdom of God, and everything else will be given you.' When the disciples asked how to pray, Jesus gave them the Our Father, which holds everything we need. And he named two places: your own room with the door shut, and a quiet place at night.",
 pic:"A path through a gate, along seven stepping stones, into a small room, and out to a hill at night.",
 q:"Follow the way Jesus taught.",
 items:[["Seek first the kingdom","Everything else will be added. Solomon asked for wisdom and was given riches too."],["The Our Father","Its seven requests hold every need."],["Your room, the door shut","One place Jesus named."],["A lonely place at night","The other, where he prayed himself."]],
 g:"Ask first for God's kingdom, in the words Jesus gave."},
/* 93 */ {t:"Preaching, and where the book stops",
 idea:"John's last subject is preaching. A sermon's power comes from the preacher's holiness more than his style. A holy life sets plain words on fire; beautiful words without spirit please the ear like music and change nothing. And then the book simply stops, mid-subject. John never finished it.",
 pic:"Two pulpits: a polished preacher and a plain one. Which sermon sets hearts alight?",
 q:"Which sermon bears fruit?",
 items:[["Fine style, no spirit","It pleases the ear like music and changes nothing."],["Plain words, a holy life","Right. Holiness sets words on fire. And here the book breaks off, unfinished."]],
 g:"A holy life speaks louder than fine words."},
/* 94 */ {t:"Union with God",
 idea:"The book never describes the top, but John's drawing does. On it he wrote: 'Only the honour and glory of God lives on this mountain.' Up here there is no marked path any more, because someone moved wholly by love doesn't need one. Around the peak he wrote the gifts found there. Faith emptied the mind, hope the memory, love the will: each 'nothing' made room for God, who is everything.",
 pic:"Light the three fires of faith, hope and love. Catch the nine gifts of the summit as they fall. Then go into the flame.",
 q:"Light the three fires, catch the gifts of the summit, then go into the flame.",
 items:[["Faith, hope and charity","Faith, hope and love emptied your mind, memory and will. Now they burn together. Across the top of his drawing John wrote: only the honour and glory of God lives on this mountain."],["The gifts of the summit","John wrote these words over the peak: peace, joy, gladness, delight, wisdom, justice, fortitude, charity, piety. You didn't chase them on the way up. They are given at the top. Beside them he wrote: here there is no more road, because for the just there is no law."],null],
 g:"Each 'nothing' on the way up made room for the God who is everything."}
];

/* John's vocabulary, for the glossary */
window.WORDS=[
 ["Night","John's name for any letting go that feels dark: giving up a craving, trusting what you cannot see, and facing a God too great for the mind."],
 ["Appetite, or desire","Not a passing feeling, but a want you have said yes to and keep hold of."],
 ["The senses","The bodily side of you: the five senses, plus imagination and feelings."],
 ["The spirit","The inner side of you: mind, memory and will."],
 ["Mind (intellect)","The power in you that understands and knows."],
 ["Memory","The power in you that keeps and recalls what you have seen, heard and felt."],
 ["Will","The power in you that loves, chooses and enjoys."],
 ["Faith, hope and charity","Three gifts of God that John pairs with mind, memory and will. Faith trusts what it cannot see. Hope reaches for what it does not yet have. Charity is love: loving God above everything."],
 ["Union with God","The goal at the top: a person whose will has nothing left in it that pulls against God's, so that God's love fills them the way sunlight fills clean glass."],
 ["Active and passive night","The active night is what you do yourself to let go; this book is about it. The passive night is what God does in you, beyond your control; John wrote about it in The Dark Night."],
 ["Meditation","Praying by picturing scenes and thinking them through, like imagining Jesus on the cross."],
 ["Contemplation","A quiet, loving attention to God that he gives, beyond pictures and thoughts."],
 ["Visions, revelations, locutions","Unusual experiences in prayer: seeing something, being shown a hidden truth, hearing words inside. John says never to cling to them."],
 ["Spiritual director, or confessor","An experienced guide a person talks to about their prayer and life."],
 ["Nada","Spanish for 'nothing'. John's word for the narrow path: wanting nothing but God."],
 ["Mortify","To deliberately starve a desire by not giving in to it."],
 ["Imperfection","A small fault you choose to keep that is not a sin, like a habit of chatter."]
];
